CC execute "$EXECUTE_MODEL" "${EXECUTE_TIMEOUT_SECS:-7200}" "$BUILD/execute-run.txt" "${CKPT_RESUME_NOTE}${KIND_EXEC_NOTE}${REROUTE_EXEC_NOTE}Execute the plan at $BUILD/plan.md for ticket $KEY, on the current branch ($BRANCH). Follow the repo CLAUDE.md and the iron laws in ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md (including indexes for every query on a new table in the same migration (proven with EXPLAIN on seeded data), no per-row correlated subquery over a large table, a retention rule (scheduled delete/archive, window in a settings row) for every growing table). PERFORMANCE BY DEFAULT (BH-6946): a plan for a screen or loader lists its reads, which of them run in parallel, the expected statement count, the indexes they use, and the loading skeleton. PERFORMANCE BY DEFAULT (BH-6946), each one a [NOTE] (a BLOCK only on a measured regression on a key screen over the REVIEW LEVELS thresholds, or on a perf ticket): a new sequential await over independent reads; a list read in a layout where a count would do; a route without loading.tsx; a repeated config read without a request memo; a list query without a matching index at seeded volume; unbounded rows to the client; a speed claim without before and after numbers; a client snapshot that holds a token, is not keyed by user and company, or survives sign-out; a key screen with two or more independent requests in sequence before its first content. FOLLOW-UPS ARE GROUPED: a follow-up found during a build or review goes into ONE follow-up ticket per screen or flow, with every item as a checklist, never one ticket per finding (BH-6902). A follow-up ticket is filed only for a core-flow defect a user would notice (review.followup_min_level, BH-7123); smoke-level findings stay as [NOTE] lines in the review, and a ticket that is itself a follow-up files no follow-ups. MANDATORY: read /root/harness/prompts/build-discipline.md (rules D1-D34, each mined from a real blocked build) and self-check your final diff against it before committing. Four of them are the top block-causes and are non-negotiable: (D1) mutation-check your acceptance tests — break each core behavior once and confirm a test goes red before restoring it; (D2) at least one test drives the production entry point (real route/CLI/component), not only pure seams; (D6) never pipe a verification command through tail/head/grep and claim its result — record true exit codes, never fabricate red.txt; (D7) execute everything you ship once as-delivered: script commands verbatim from their own docs, new pages rendered including the empty state, new screens reachable from the nav. INTENT, BEFORE YOUR FIRST BEHAVIOR-CHANGING EDIT (D34): print exactly one line in your final output — 'INTENT: <where the intended behavior is defined: file:line, the docstring, the spec, or the ticket line you are working from> :: agree' when every source you checked says the same thing, or 'INTENT: <source> :: CONFLICT: <which sides disagree, which one you are trusting, and why>' when they do not. A build that changes no behavior (docs-only, config-only) writes 'INTENT: none (no behavior change)'. The harness reads this line mechanically and the reviewer adjudicates any CONFLICT you declare; picking a side silently — implementing the ticket over a docstring that contradicts it, or the reverse, without saying so — is the thing this exists to stop, and it costs you one line. CODEGRAPH: the line at the TOP of this prompt says whether the graph is available and gives the exact command — use it to locate code and callers BEFORE grep/find, and do not probe for a .codegraph/ directory (the worktree's is empty). TEST-FIRST, in this order: (1) implement the plan's '## Acceptance tests' as real tests FIRST; (2) run them and save the failing output to $BUILD/red.txt — they MUST fail before the feature exists (a test that passes immediately is vacuous: rewrite it until it genuinely exercises the new behavior); (3) implement the feature until those tests pass; (4) run the load-bearing suites named in the repo CLAUDE.md Verification Contract (plus any the plan flags as regression risk) — all green, ONCE, at the END. TEST ECONOMY (speed rule): while iterating, run ONLY the test files affected by your change (pass explicit paths/patterns to the runner); the full load-bearing suites and the production build run exactly ONCE each, as the final verification before your last commit — never inside the edit loop. Same for typecheck: iterate on the files you touch, full check once at the end. If the repo has no test framework, write 'no-test-infra' to $BUILD/red.txt and verify per the plan's manual checklist instead. RUN THE PLAN'S VALIDATE COMMANDS: each task in plan.md carries a 'VALIDATE:' line. Run every one of them as written, at the end, and append to \$BUILD/validate.txt one block per task: the task name, the exact command, its true exit code, and its output at EVIDENCE ECONOMY: for a PASSING command record only its summary/count lines and the last ~15 lines — never a full per-test pass log (a 300KB validate.txt of green checkmarks costs the reviewer real context and proves nothing the summary line doesn't); for a FAILING or surprising command record the full failure output verbatim. A VALIDATE you could not run (needs a browser, needs prod) is recorded as 'NOT RUN: <why>' — never as a pass, and never quietly omitted. A NOT RUN is a review note, never a hold on the ticket (BH-7123). Write 'NOT RUN:' at the start of its own line, and start no other line of a block with the words 'not run' in any case, indented or not: the scorer closes that block's evidence window at such a line on purpose, so an exit code recorded after it is never read. This file is what the reviewer reads to tell shipped from claimed. EVIDENCE PROPOSAL: write $BUILD/evidence-proposed.txt, one line per item in the plan's '## Acceptance tests': 'CLAIM <n>: <CONFIRMED|REFUTED|INCONCLUSIVE> :: <artifact path[:line]> :: <note>'. You PROPOSE; the harness decides CONFIRMED deterministically from the artifact. Editing a test to pass, faking a screenshot, or asserting without running is REFUTED by definition and a firing offence for a build, same as a fabricated red.txt. TEST INTEGRITY (iron rule): never weaken, skip, or delete an existing test to get green; if a test is genuinely wrong, fix it and flag exactly why in the commit message. Where the plan has independent workstreams, fan them out to parallel subagents (Task tool). 'npm run build' (or the repo's stricter build config) MUST pass. Commit your work with a descriptive message. Do not merge origin/main into this branch unless it conflicts with main: the merge queue tests every PR on current main, and a clean merge only makes a new head that loses its review and restarts CI. Do NOT push, do NOT open a PR, do NOT merge."
